' Fictitious capital: class model
' Companion notes: notes/fictitious-capital.md — full stereotype legend: notes/stereotypes.md
'
' Paper titles (shares, government bonds) are capitalised claims on future
' surplus value or future tax. They are not a second existence of the real
' capital invested in production (Marx, Capital III, ch. 29–30; SPGB).

@startuml fictitious-capital
title VI. Fictitious capital — paper claims on future surplus value
caption Titles are not a second real capital: notes/fictitious-capital.md

hide circle
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam backgroundColor white
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam packageStyle rectangle
skinparam ranksep 50
skinparam nodesep 35
skinparam class {
  BackgroundColor<<abstract det.>> #E3F2FD
  BackgroundColor<<social relation>> #EDE7F6
  BackgroundColor<<historically specific>> #FFEBEE
}

package "I. Real capital still produces surplus value" as P1 {
  class SurplusValue <<historically specific>> <<social relation>> {
    unpaid labour
    (full definition: surplus-value-capital.puml)
  }
  class RealCapital <<historically specific>> <<social relation>> {
    money invested in MP + LP
    circuit : M – C … P … C' – M'
    the only capital that produces s
  }
  class JointStockCompany <<historically specific>> {
    raises funds once by issuing titles
    invests proceeds as real capital
  }
}

package "II. Capitalisation (how a title gets a price)" as P2 {
  abstract class FictitiousCapital <<abstract det.>> <<social relation>> <<historically specific>> {
    {abstract}
    capitalised claim on future revenue
    price = expected income / rate of interest
    not a second real capital
  }
  class Capitalisation <<historically specific>> {
    treats a regular income
    as if it were interest on a principal
  }
  class RateOfInterest <<historically specific>> <<social relation>> {
    going rate at which income
    is capitalised
    (full definition: finance.puml Interest)
  }
}

package "III. Titles" as P3 {
  class Share <<historically specific>> {
    title to a portion of
    future surplus value
    dividend + hoped-for capital gain
  }
  class GovernmentBond <<historically specific>> {
    title to future tax
    doubly fictitious:
    the money has already been spent
  }
  class Tax <<historically specific>> {
    deduction from social surplus value
    (or from wages — still not new value)
    (the state that levies: state.puml)
  }
}

package "IV. Secondary market" as P4 {
  class StockExchange <<historically specific>> {
    market in titles, not in factories
    circulation of property rights
  }
  class CapitalGain <<historically specific>> {
    price difference on resale of a title
    a transfer, not new value
  }
  class SpeculativeBubble <<historically specific>> {
    title prices detach from
    the movement of real capital
    bursts when claims cannot be validated
  }
}

class PaperDuplicateIllusion <<abstract det.>> {
  {abstract}
  rejected claim: the title is a second
  real capital beside the enterprise
}

JointStockCompany --> RealCapital : invests proceeds as
RealCapital *-- SurplusValue : appropriates
JointStockCompany --> Share : issues once
Share --|> FictitiousCapital
GovernmentBond --|> FictitiousCapital
FictitiousCapital --> Capitalisation : formed by
Capitalisation --> RateOfInterest : divides income by
Share --> SurplusValue : claims a portion of future
GovernmentBond --> Tax : claims future
Tax --> SurplusValue : deducted from
StockExchange --> Share : circulates
StockExchange --> GovernmentBond : circulates
StockExchange --> CapitalGain : redistributes as
CapitalGain ..> SurplusValue : does not produce
SpeculativeBubble --> FictitiousCapital : inflates the price of
SpeculativeBubble ..> RealCapital : detaches from\n(temporarily)
PaperDuplicateIllusion ..> Share : mistakes title for
PaperDuplicateIllusion ..> GovernmentBond : mistakes title for
FictitiousCapital ..> PaperDuplicateIllusion : is not
RealCapital ..> PaperDuplicateIllusion : money does not\nexist twice

P1 -[hidden]right-> P2
P2 -[hidden]right-> P3
P1 -[hidden]down-> P4
P3 -[hidden]down-> P4

note as NOnce
  **Money does not exist twice (Capital III, ch. 29)**
  The sum advanced to an enterprise exists
  as real capital in production. The share
  is only a title to a corresponding portion
  of the surplus value to be realised.
  A rise or fall in the paper's price,
  independent of that real capital, leaves
  the wealth of the nation unchanged.
end note

note bottom of GovernmentBond
  Doubly fictitious: the state has spent
  the borrowed money (often unproductively);
  the bond is a capitalised claim on future
  tax, itself a share of surplus value.
end note

note right of SpeculativeBubble
  The credit system that finances industry
  also finances the market in titles.
  Periodically: "money-making without
  the mediation of the production process"
  (Capital II, ch. 1). The bubble bursts.
end note

@enduml
